// Unit 3: Options. Chapter 3: Options Strategies.
export default {
  id: 'options-ch3',
  title: 'Options Strategies',
  blurb: 'From covered calls to iron condors: how real strategies are built, what they risk, and the danger zone every beginner should understand first.',
  lessons: [
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1
    {
      id: 'options-3-long-options-done-right',
      title: 'Buying Calls & Puts the Smart Way',
      summary: `How to think through strike, expiration and position size before buying an option, and why cheap short-dated options are usually long shots.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: `Dev's weekly habit`, html: `<p>Every Monday, Dev scrolls the chain for the cheapest calls he can find, usually far out of the money and expiring Friday. A $0.10 call costs $10, so he buys twenty. Most weeks they expire worthless. Once in a while a stock rips and he makes ten times his money, and he tells everyone about that week.</p><p>Add it up over a few months, though, and Dev is down. The rare wins do not cover the steady losses. He is not unlucky. He is paying a fair price, or worse after spreads and fees, for bets that usually lose.</p><p>Buying options is not bad by itself. Defined risk, leverage and protection are real benefits. But buying them <em>well</em> means making deliberate choices about strike, expiration and size, instead of grabbing whatever looks cheapest. That is this lesson.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `Cheap is a price, not a strategy. Let us build a real plan instead.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Three questions before any long option', html: `<p>When you buy stock, you only need to be right about direction, eventually. When you buy an option, you need to be right about three things at once:</p><ol><li><strong>Direction:</strong> up for calls, down for puts.</li><li><strong>Size:</strong> the stock must move past your breakeven, not just a little in your favor.</li><li><strong>Timing:</strong> the move must happen before expiration, ideally before time decay eats most of your premium.</li></ol><p>Miss any one of the three and the trade can lose. The stock can go your way, but too little. It can go your way by plenty, but a week after your option expired.</p><p>So before buying, write down your actual forecast: "I think LMNB reaches about $56 within six weeks." That one sentence tells you which strike and expiration make sense, and makes it obvious if the trade cannot work.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Choosing a strike', html: `<p>LMNB is at <strong>$50</strong>. Here are calls expiring in about 60 days:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Strike</th><th>Premium</th><th>Delta</th><th>Breakeven</th><th>Move needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>45 (ITM)</td><td>$6.60</td><td>0.80</td><td>$51.60</td><td><span class="up">+3.2%</span></td></tr><tr><td>50 (ATM)</td><td>$3.30</td><td>0.55</td><td>$53.30</td><td><span class="up">+6.6%</span></td></tr><tr><td>55 (OTM)</td><td>$1.40</td><td>0.30</td><td>$56.40</td><td><span class="up">+12.8%</span></td></tr><tr><td>60 (far OTM)</td><td>$0.50</td><td>0.12</td><td>$60.50</td><td><span class="up">+21%</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The ITM call costs the most but needs the smallest move and acts most like the stock. The far OTM call is cheap but needs a 21% rally in two months. Neither is "better." Each matches a different forecast. If you expect a modest move, a far OTM call is the wrong tool even if your direction is right.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `LMNB is at $50. A 55 call costs $1.00. By what percentage must LMNB rise to reach the breakeven at expiration?`, answer: 12, tolerance: 0.2, unit: '%', explain: `Breakeven is $55 + $1 = $56. From $50 to $56 is a $6 rise, and $6 / $50 = 12%.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Choosing an expiration', html: `<p>Time costs money, but too little time is often the more expensive mistake.</p><p>Leo expects LMNB to rise after a product launch in about a month. A 30-day option would leave him no room if the launch slips a week. A 90-day option costs more up front, but its daily time decay is slower, and he can sell it after the launch with plenty of extrinsic value left.</p><p>A common approach among option educators: buy <strong>more time than you think you need</strong>, and plan to exit before the final few weeks, when the decay curve steepens. That way, a move that comes late is still a winner instead of a worthless expiration.</p><p>And watch the calendar for scheduled events. If earnings fall inside your expiration window, you are paying for elevated implied volatility that will likely be crushed after the report.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The short-dated OTM trap', html: `<p>Back to Dev. LMNB is at $50 and he buys a <strong>58 call expiring in one week for $0.15</strong>, or $15.</p><p>Breakeven: $58.15. LMNB must rise more than <strong>16%</strong> in five trading days. The option's delta is around 0.05, meaning the market sees roughly a one-in-twenty shot of it even finishing in the money, let alone past breakeven.</p><p>These options feel safe because the dollar amount is small. But the probability is smaller. Spending $15 a week on long shots is $780 a year, and spreads often take an extra bite on the way in and out.</p><p>There is also a psychological trap. A rare 10x win feels like proof of skill, which encourages bigger bets on the same low odds. Casinos use this same pattern, and it works on smart people too.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `A one-week far OTM option is closer to a lottery ticket than an investment. The odds are printed right there in the delta.` } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'call', side: 'long', strike: 58, premium: 0.15, spot: 50 }, caption: `This is Dev's 58 call. Slide the price and see how far LMNB must travel from $50 before the line turns green.` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Which purchase is the lowest-probability bet?`, options: [`A 45 call with 90 days left, stock at $50`, `A 50 call with 60 days left, stock at $50`, `A 60 call with 5 days left, stock at $50`, `A 55 call with 120 days left, stock at $50`], answer: 2, explain: `It needs a 20% rally in five days. Far out of the money plus very little time is the least likely combination to pay off.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Sizing: the premium is your stop', html: `<p>The best thing about buying options is that your maximum loss is known: the premium. The worst habit is ignoring that and betting too much of it.</p><p>A common risk-management guideline is to risk only a small, fixed slice of your account on any one trade, often 1% to 2%. With a long option, assume the whole premium could go to zero, because often it does.</p><p>Leo has a $5,000 account. At 2%, his maximum risk per trade is $100. That means one contract priced at $1.00, not five. Dev, meanwhile, put $2,500 into weekly calls in a single week. One bad week cost him half his account.</p><p>Small sizing feels boring. It is also what keeps you in the game long enough to learn anything.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'bolt', mood: 'think', say: `Rule check: max premium per trade equals account size times your risk percent. Compute it before you open the chain.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz has an $8,000 account and risks at most 2% per trade. The call she wants costs $0.75. What is the maximum number of contracts she can buy within her limit?`, answer: 2, tolerance: 0, unit: '', explain: `2% of $8,000 is $160. Each contract costs $0.75 x 100 = $75. Two contracts cost $150. Three would cost $225, over her limit.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Exit rules before entry', html: `<p>Decide how you will get out before you get in. Three exits cover most situations:</p><ul><li><strong>Profit target:</strong> for example, sell when the option doubles, or when the stock reaches your forecast price.</li><li><strong>Loss limit:</strong> for example, sell if the option loses half its value, rather than riding it to zero.</li><li><strong>Time stop:</strong> sell by a set date, such as three weeks before expiration, whatever the result.</li></ul><p>Then check the <strong>liquidity</strong>. An option with tight bid-ask spreads and healthy open interest is easier to exit at a fair price. If you can only sell at a terrible bid, your exit plan is weaker than it looks.</p><p><strong>Puts follow the same logic.</strong> Bearish bets need direction, size and timing too, and put buyers face one extra headwind: stock markets have historically risen over long periods, and puts tend to get expensive right after sharp drops, when everyone wants protection.</p>` },
        { type: 'order', prompt: `Put a disciplined long-option process in order.`, items: [`Write down your forecast for direction, size and timing`, `Pick an expiration with time to spare`, `Pick a strike that fits the expected move`, `Size the trade so losing the full premium is acceptable`, `Set profit, loss and time exits before entering`], explain: `The forecast comes first because it decides everything else. Sizing and exit rules come before you click buy, not after the trade moves.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Which three things must an option buyer be right about?`, options: [`Direction, size of the move, and timing`, `Direction, dividends, and volume`, `Strike, broker, and fees`, `Only direction`], answer: 0, explain: `An option can lose if the move is too small or too late, even when the direction is right.` },
          { q: `LMNB is at $40. A 44 call costs $0.50. What is the breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$40.50`, `$43.50`, `$44.00`, `$44.50`], answer: 3, explain: `Strike plus premium: $44 + $0.50 = $44.50.` },
          { q: `Why are short-dated, far out-of-the-money calls usually poor bets?`, options: [`They cannot be sold early`, `They need a large move in very little time, so they usually expire worthless`, `They have unlimited risk`, `They are not allowed in cash accounts`], answer: 1, explain: `Low delta signals low odds. The small price reflects that.` },
          { q: `Leo has $12,000 and risks 1% per trade. What is the most premium he should spend on one long option trade?`, options: [`$12`, `$1,200`, `$120`, `$600`], answer: 2, explain: `1% of $12,000 is $120. Since the premium can go to zero, that is his maximum spend.` },
          { q: `Why might a buyer choose more time than the expected move needs?`, options: [`Longer options never lose value`, `More time means lower premium`, `Long-dated options are always cheaper per day and never decay`, `It leaves room for a delayed move and avoids the steepest decay at the end`], answer: 3, explain: `Extra time costs more, but slows daily decay and protects against being right too late.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
    {
      id: 'options-3-covered-calls',
      title: 'Covered Calls: Renting Out Your Shares',
      summary: `Own 100 shares, sell a call against them, and collect premium. Learn exactly what you gain, what you give up, and the math of every outcome.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'coin',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: `Ms. Ortiz's idle shares`, html: `<p>Ms. Ortiz owns <strong>100 shares of LMNB</strong>, bought at <strong>$50</strong>. She likes the company long term, but she does not expect much action over the next couple of months. She would also be fine selling at $55.</p><p>So she sells one <strong>LMNB 55 call</strong> expiring in about 45 days, for <strong>$1.20</strong>. That puts <span class="up">$120</span> in her account immediately, and it is hers to keep no matter what happens.</p><p>In exchange, she has promised to sell her 100 shares at $55 if the call is exercised. Because she already owns the shares, the promise is <em>covered</em>: she can always deliver. That is a <span class="term" data-def="Owning 100 shares and selling one call against them to collect premium, in exchange for capping upside at the strike.">covered call</span>.</p><p>Think of it as renting out the upside of her shares above $55. The renter pays $120 for the chance that LMNB rallies past $55.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Covered call: own the shares, sell someone the right to buy them higher. Collect rent while you wait.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'covered-call', caption: `Stock plus a short call: the line rises with the stock until the strike, then goes flat. The premium shifts the whole line up a little.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Every outcome at expiration', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at expiration</th><th>Shares</th><th>Call premium</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$40</td><td>-$1,000</td><td>+$120</td><td><span class="down">-$880</span></td></tr><tr><td>$48.80</td><td>-$120</td><td>+$120</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><td>$50</td><td>$0</td><td>+$120</td><td><span class="up">+$120</span></td></tr><tr><td>$55</td><td>+$500</td><td>+$120</td><td><span class="up">+$620</span></td></tr><tr><td>$65</td><td>+$500 (sold at $55)</td><td>+$120</td><td><span class="up">+$620</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three formulas, all per share, then times 100:</p><ul><li><span class="hl">Max profit = (strike - stock cost) + premium</span> = $5 + $1.20 = $6.20, so <strong>$620</strong>.</li><li><span class="hl">Breakeven = stock cost - premium</span> = <strong>$48.80</strong>.</li><li><span class="hl">Max loss = stock cost - premium</span>, if the stock goes to zero: <strong>$4,880</strong>.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo owns 100 shares bought at $30 and sells a 33 call for $0.90. What is his maximum profit at expiration, in dollars?`, answer: 390, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `($33 - $30) + $0.90 = $3.90 per share, times 100 = $390. Above $33 his shares are called away, so gains stop there.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'What you give up', html: `<p>Look at the $65 row again. Without the call, Ms. Ortiz would have made $1,500 on her shares. With it, she made $620. The extra $880 went to the call buyer. That is the core trade-off: <span class="hl">a covered call swaps big upside for a small, certain payment</span>.</p><p>And look at the $40 row. The call softened the loss by just $120. A covered call is <strong>not</strong> downside protection in any meaningful sense. If the stock crashes, you hold almost the full loss, minus a little premium.</p><p>So covered calls tend to work best when the stock goes sideways or rises modestly. They work worst in two cases: a big rally (you miss most of it) and a big drop (you take most of it). Before selling one, ask yourself honestly: would I be okay selling my shares at this strike?</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `The premium is small and certain. The upside you give away can be big. The downside you keep is almost all of it.` } },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'call', side: 'short', strike: 55, premium: 1.2, spot: 50 }, caption: `This shows the short 55 call on its own. A covered call adds 100 shares to it, which turns the unlimited loss above $55 into a flat, capped profit.` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz bought shares at $50 and sold a 55 call for $1.20. What is her breakeven stock price at expiration?`, answer: 48.8, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `Stock cost minus premium: $50 - $1.20 = $48.80. The premium cushions the first $1.20 of a decline.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Picking the strike and date', html: `<p>Strike choice is a dial between income and upside:</p><ul><li><strong>Closer strike (say 52):</strong> bigger premium, but a higher chance the shares get called away and a lower cap.</li><li><strong>Further strike (say 60):</strong> smaller premium, more room to rise, lower chance of being called away.</li></ul><p>Expiration matters too. Shorter-dated calls decay faster, which helps sellers, but need replacing more often, with more trading costs and more decisions. Many covered call sellers use roughly one to two months, but there is no magic number.</p><p>If the stock rallies toward the strike and the seller wants to keep the shares, they can <strong>roll</strong>: buy back the current call (often at a loss) and sell a new one with a later date and higher strike. Rolling is a new trade, not a free fix. Sometimes it just postpones accepting that the shares should be sold.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Ms. Ortiz's main goal is to keep her shares through a possible big rally. Which covered call choice fits best?`, options: [`Sell a call far above the current price, accepting less premium`, `Sell a call just above the current price for maximum premium`, `Sell two calls per 100 shares`, `Sell an in-the-money call`], answer: 0, explain: `A far strike leaves more upside and lowers the chance of being called away. The trade-off is a smaller premium.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Assignment and dividends', html: `<p>Covered calls on most stocks are American style, so assignment can come early. It usually happens when the call is in the money and has little extrinsic value left, and it is especially common the day before an <strong>ex-dividend date</strong>, when call holders exercise to capture the dividend.</p><p>For Ms. Ortiz, early assignment is not a disaster. She sells her shares at $55, which she already agreed to, and keeps the $120. She just misses the dividend she might have expected.</p><p>The real surprise is for people who sell calls on shares they later sell separately, or who sell more calls than they have shares to cover. That turns part of the position into a <strong>naked call</strong>, with theoretically unlimited risk. One contract per 100 shares, always.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Covered calls are usually the first strategy brokers allow, because the shares fully cover the obligation.` } },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'myth', title: 'Myth: covered calls are free money', html: `<p>The premium is not free. It is payment for the upside you hand over. Historically, benchmark indexes that track covered call strategies on the S&amp;P 500 have tended to lag the plain index during strong bull markets, because the best rallies get capped, and held up a little better in flat or falling stretches. Know which trade-off you are making.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Annualized yield can mislead', html: `<p>Collecting $120 on $5,000 is 2.4% in 45 days, which some tools will annualize to nearly 20% a year. That math assumes you can repeat it eight times a year with no losses on the shares. Real results include stock drops, called-away rallies and trading costs. Judge the trade by the total position, not the premium alone.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Covered call cheat sheet', cards: [
          { front: 'Max profit', back: `Strike minus stock cost, plus premium, times 100.` },
          { front: 'Breakeven', back: `Stock cost minus premium.` },
          { front: 'Max loss', back: `Stock cost minus premium, times 100, if the stock goes to zero.` },
          { front: 'Best case', back: `Stock finishes right at or slightly above the strike.` },
          { front: 'Rolling', back: `Buying back the current call and selling a new one with a later date, often a higher strike.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A covered call protects the shareholder from most of the loss if the stock falls sharply.`, answer: false, explain: `The premium only offsets a small part of a decline. The shareholder still carries nearly the full downside of owning the stock.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A covered call consists of...`, options: [`Owning 100 shares and buying a call`, `Owning 100 shares and selling a call`, `Selling a call without shares`, `Buying a put and selling a call`], answer: 1, explain: `The shares cover the obligation created by the short call.` },
          { q: `Leo buys 100 shares at $20 and sells a 22 call for $0.60. His max profit at expiration is...`, options: [`$60`, `$200`, `$260`, `Unlimited`], answer: 2, explain: `($22 - $20) + $0.60 = $2.60 per share, times 100 = $260.` },
          { q: `In which scenario does a covered call writer feel the most regret compared with just holding the stock?`, options: [`The stock goes sideways`, `The stock falls slightly`, `The stock stays exactly at the purchase price`, `The stock rallies far above the strike`], answer: 3, explain: `Gains above the strike go to the call buyer, so a big rally is where the cap hurts most.` },
          { q: `Stock bought at $80, 85 call sold for $2.00. Breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$78`, `$82`, `$83`, `$87`], answer: 0, explain: `Stock cost minus premium: $80 - $2 = $78.` },
          { q: `When is early assignment on a covered call most common?`, options: [`Right after selling it`, `When it is far out of the money`, `The day before an ex-dividend date, when the call is in the money`, `On the first trading day of the month`], answer: 2, explain: `Holders exercise in-the-money calls to collect the dividend when it is worth more than the remaining extrinsic value.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
    {
      id: 'options-3-cash-secured-puts-wheel',
      title: 'Cash-Secured Puts & the Wheel',
      summary: `Sell a put on a stock you would like to own, get paid while you wait, and learn how the popular "wheel" cycle works, including where it breaks.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'piggy',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Getting paid to wait', html: `<p>Leo wants to own LMNB, but at $50 it feels a bit pricey. He would happily buy at <strong>$45</strong>.</p><p>He could place a limit order to buy at $45 and wait. Or he could <strong>sell a 45 put</strong> expiring in about 45 days for <strong>$1.00</strong>, collecting <span class="up">$100</span> right away. If LMNB falls below $45 by expiration, he will likely be assigned and buy 100 shares at $45, which is what he wanted anyway. If it does not, he keeps the $100.</p><p>To make sure he can pay, he sets aside <strong>$4,500</strong> in cash, enough to buy 100 shares at the strike. That makes it a <span class="term" data-def="Selling a put while holding enough cash to buy the shares at the strike if assigned.">cash-secured put</span>. Brokers typically hold that cash as collateral, and many allow cash-secured puts at one of their lower option approval levels.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'penny', mood: 'happy', say: `Set the cash aside, name your price, and collect a little while you wait. I respect anyone who keeps the cash ready.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Every outcome at expiration', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at expiration</th><th>What happens</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$55</td><td>Put expires</td><td><span class="up">+$100</span>, no shares</td></tr><tr><td>$45</td><td>Put expires (at the money)</td><td><span class="up">+$100</span>, no shares</td></tr><tr><td>$44</td><td>Assigned at $45</td><td>Shares worth $4,400, cost $4,500, plus $100: $0</td></tr><tr><td>$40</td><td>Assigned at $45</td><td><span class="down">-$400</span> on paper</td></tr><tr><td>$30</td><td>Assigned at $45</td><td><span class="down">-$1,400</span> on paper</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><span class="hl">Effective cost basis if assigned = strike - premium</span> = $44.</li><li><span class="hl">Breakeven = strike - premium</span> = $44.</li><li><span class="hl">Max profit = premium</span> = $100.</li><li><span class="hl">Max loss = (strike - premium) x 100</span> = $4,400, if LMNB goes to zero.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz sells a 60 put for $2.50 and is assigned. What is her effective cost per share?`, answer: 57.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `She pays the $60 strike but already collected $2.50, so her effective cost is $60 - $2.50 = $57.50.` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'put', side: 'short', strike: 45, premium: 1, spot: 50 }, caption: `Leo's short 45 put. The profit is capped at $100 above $45, while losses grow as the stock falls below the $44 breakeven.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Cash-secured put vs a limit order', html: `<p>Both approaches aim to buy LMNB at $45. Here is how they differ:</p><ul><li><strong>Stock drifts to $47:</strong> the limit order never fills and earns nothing. The put seller keeps $100.</li><li><strong>Stock dips to $44 midweek, then rallies to $55:</strong> the limit order may fill at $45 and ride the rally. The put seller probably is not assigned, keeps $100, and misses the rally.</li><li><strong>Stock crashes to $30:</strong> both end up owning shares near $45. The put seller's cost is $1 lower thanks to the premium.</li></ul><p>A cash-secured put is not magic. It earns a premium in exchange for giving up flexibility: you are committed to buy at $45 until expiration, even if bad news makes $45 look terrible, unless you pay to buy the put back.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo collects $100 for a put while setting aside $4,500. What return is that on the cash set aside, in percent? Round to one decimal.`, answer: 2.2, tolerance: 0.1, unit: '%', explain: `$100 / $4,500 = about 2.2% for the period. Note that this ignores any interest the cash may earn and any loss if he is assigned.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Choosing the strike', html: `<p>The strike is the price you are promising to pay, so pick it like a buy price, not like a premium target.</p><ul><li><strong>Strike near the current price:</strong> bigger premium, higher chance of assignment, less of a discount.</li><li><strong>Strike well below the price:</strong> smaller premium, lower chance of assignment, bigger discount if it happens.</li></ul><p>The put's delta gives a rough sense of the odds. A 45 put with a delta of -0.25 has, very roughly, a one-in-four chance of finishing in the money.</p><p>Watch out for premiums that look unusually rich. A put paying far more than similar stocks usually means the market expects a big move, often an earnings report, a lawsuit or a drug trial result inside your window. High premium is the market warning you, not rewarding you.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'What happens to the cash', html: `<p>The $4,500 is tied up until the put expires, is assigned or is bought back. Depending on the broker, the collateral may still earn interest, or the broker may let it sit in certain cash-like holdings. Rules differ, so check yours. Either way, you cannot spend that cash on anything else while the put is open.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The wheel', html: `<p>Some traders chain cash-secured puts and covered calls into a loop called <strong>the wheel</strong>:</p><ol><li>Sell a cash-secured put on a stock you want to own.</li><li>If it expires, collect the premium and sell another put.</li><li>If assigned, you now own 100 shares at an effective discount.</li><li>Sell covered calls on those shares.</li><li>If the shares are called away, you are back to cash. Start again.</li></ol><p>On paper it is a tidy machine that collects premium at every step. In practice, it inherits every risk of owning the stock, plus a cap on the upside. The premiums are small compared with how much a stock can fall.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'think', say: `The wheel sounds like a perpetual money machine. It is really stock ownership with a few small coupons attached.` } },
        { type: 'order', prompt: `Put the steps of one full turn of the wheel in order.`, items: [`Sell a cash-secured put`, `Get assigned and buy 100 shares`, `Sell a covered call on the shares`, `Shares get called away at the call strike`, `Back to cash, ready to sell another put`], explain: `Short put, assignment, covered call, called away, and back to cash. Each step collects a premium.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Where the wheel breaks', html: `<p>Suppose Leo is assigned at $45 and LMNB keeps falling to <strong>$32</strong> after a bad earnings report.</p><p>Now his covered call options are ugly. A call near his $44 cost basis pays almost nothing because it is far out of the money. A call near $32 pays more, but if it gets exercised he sells at $32 and <strong>locks in a $12-per-share loss</strong>. Many wheel traders end up stuck holding a fallen stock for months, collecting tiny premiums while hoping for a recovery that may not come.</p><p>Other honest limitations:</p><ul><li>Each contract needs cash for 100 shares, so one position on a $200 stock ties up about $20,000.</li><li>It concentrates you in a handful of stocks, often the volatile ones with juicy premiums.</li><li>Big rallies get capped twice: puts only collect a premium, and covered calls cap gains.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Only sell puts on stocks you would truly be glad to own at the strike, through bad news. Juicy premiums usually mean scary risk.` } },
        { type: 'check', question: `Leo was assigned LMNB at an effective $44. It now trades at $32. Why is selling a 32 call risky for him?`, options: [`If exercised, he sells at $32 and locks in a loss of about $12 per share`, `It would be a naked call`, `Calls cannot be sold on assigned shares`, `It pays a larger premium than he needs`], answer: 0, explain: `A covered call strike below your cost basis turns a paper loss into a real one if the shares are called away.` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Put-selling vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Cash-secured put', back: `A short put backed by enough cash to buy the shares at the strike.` },
          { front: 'Effective cost basis', back: `Strike minus premium received: your real purchase price if assigned.` },
          { front: 'Collateral', back: `Cash the broker sets aside so you can meet the obligation.` },
          { front: 'The wheel', back: `Repeating cycle of cash-secured puts, assignment, covered calls and called-away shares.` },
          { front: 'Buy to close', back: `Buying back a short option to end the obligation early, at today's price.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A cash-secured put seller keeps the full premium even if the stock rallies 30%.`, answer: true, explain: `The put expires worthless and the seller keeps the premium. But that premium is all they earn. They miss the rally entirely.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `What makes a put "cash-secured"?`, options: [`The broker insures it`, `The seller holds enough cash to buy the shares at the strike`, `It is bought with cash, not margin`, `It is exercisable for cash only`], answer: 1, explain: `The cash covers the obligation to buy 100 shares at the strike if assigned.` },
          { q: `Ms. Ortiz sells a 30 put for $0.80. Her breakeven at expiration is...`, options: [`$30.80`, `$30.00`, `$29.20`, `$29.80`], answer: 2, explain: `Strike minus premium: $30 - $0.80 = $29.20.` },
          { q: `How much cash secures one 70 put?`, options: [`$700`, `$70`, `The premium amount`, `$7,000`], answer: 3, explain: `$70 strike x 100 shares = $7,000.` },
          { q: `Which is a real downside of the wheel?`, options: [`If the stock falls a lot, you hold the loss and calls near your cost pay little`, `It cannot lose money`, `It requires no cash`, `Premiums are taxed at zero`], answer: 0, explain: `The wheel inherits the stock's downside. Small premiums do not offset a large decline.` },
          { q: `Compared with a limit order to buy at the strike, a cash-secured put...`, options: [`Always gets better prices`, `Earns a premium but locks you into the obligation until expiration or buyback`, `Has no risk if the stock falls`, `Guarantees you will own the shares`], answer: 1, explain: `You get paid for the commitment, but you cannot simply cancel it like a limit order.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4
    {
      id: 'options-3-protective-puts-collars',
      title: 'Protective Puts & Collars',
      summary: `Insure a stock position with a put, then learn the collar, which pays for that insurance by giving up some upside.`,
      minutes: 14,
      icon: 'shield',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: `Leo's nervous quarter`, html: `<p>Leo owns <strong>100 shares of LMNB</strong>, bought at <strong>$50</strong>. He plans to hold for years, but the next few months look bumpy: an earnings report, a product recall rumor, a shaky market.</p><p>He does not want to sell. He just wants a floor. So he buys a <strong>protective put</strong>: one LMNB <strong>45 put</strong> expiring in about three months, for <strong>$1.50</strong>, or $150.</p><p>For the next three months, no matter what happens, Leo can sell his shares for $45. If the recall rumor turns out true and LMNB drops to $30, his put lets him walk away at $45. If nothing bad happens, the put expires and he is out $150, the cost of sleeping well.</p><p>When someone buys the stock and the put at the same time, traders sometimes call it a <strong>married put</strong>. Same shape, same math.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `A protective put is car insurance for your shares. Pay a premium, get a guaranteed sale price for a while.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'protective-put', caption: `Stock plus a long put: unlimited upside minus the premium, with the downside cut off flat at the put's strike.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The math of a floor', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>LMNB at expiration</th><th>Shares</th><th>Put</th><th>Total (after $150 cost)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$30</td><td>-$2,000</td><td>+$1,500</td><td><span class="down">-$650</span></td></tr><tr><td>$45</td><td>-$500</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$650</span></td></tr><tr><td>$50</td><td>$0</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="down">-$150</span></td></tr><tr><td>$51.50</td><td>+$150</td><td>$0</td><td>$0</td></tr><tr><td>$65</td><td>+$1,500</td><td>$0</td><td><span class="up">+$1,350</span></td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li><span class="hl">Max loss = (stock cost - put strike + premium) x 100</span> = ($5 + $1.50) x 100 = <strong>$650</strong>.</li><li><span class="hl">Breakeven = stock cost + premium</span> = <strong>$51.50</strong>.</li><li><strong>Max profit:</strong> unlimited, minus the $150.</li></ul>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz owns 100 shares bought at $80 and buys a 75 put for $2.00. What is her maximum loss at expiration, in dollars?`, answer: 700, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$80 - $75 = $5 drop to the floor, plus $2 premium = $7 per share, times 100 = $700.` },
        { type: 'widget', name: 'option-payoff', props: { kind: 'put', side: 'long', strike: 45, premium: 1.5, spot: 50 }, caption: `This is the put on its own. Add 100 shares to it in your head: the put's gains below $45 cancel the stock's losses, creating a floor.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Puts vs stop-loss orders', html: `<p>Why not just set a stop-loss order at $45 for free?</p><p>A stop-loss becomes a market order once the price is hit. If LMNB closes at $48 and opens the next morning at <strong>$36</strong> after bad news, the stop sells near $36, not $45. Stocks can <span class="term" data-def="Open at a price far from the previous close, skipping every price in between.">gap</span> right through a stop. Stops can also trigger on a brief dip, kicking you out right before a rebound.</p><p>A put does not care about gaps. It guarantees the right to sell at $45 until expiration, no matter where the stock opens. That guarantee is what the premium buys.</p><p>The trade-offs: puts cost money, and the protection ends at expiration. A stop is free but can fail exactly when you need it most.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `A stop-loss is a request to sell near a price. A put is a contract to sell at a price. Overnight gaps reveal the difference.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Insurance is not cheap forever', html: `<p>Leo's put cost $1.50 for three months, which is 3% of the stock's $50 value. If he bought similar protection every quarter for a year, that could cost around 12% a year, if prices stayed similar. That is a huge drag on a stock that might only return a fraction of that.</p><p>This is why most long-term investors do not hedge constantly. Protective puts tend to make the most sense for specific, temporary worries: a big event, a concentrated position you cannot sell yet, or a period when a big loss would be truly painful.</p><p>Strike choice is the deductible again. A 48 put protects more but costs more. A 40 put is cheap but only kicks in after a 20% drop. And remember from the IV lessons: protection costs most right after markets fall, when everyone wants it.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Picking how long to insure', html: `<p>The expiration decides how long the floor lasts. Remember the square-root rule from the theta lesson: an at-the-money option with six times the time costs only about 2.4 times as much, because the square root of 6 is about 2.45. So longer protection costs more in total, but less per month.</p><p>That suggests two common approaches:</p><ul><li><strong>Event insurance:</strong> a short-dated put that covers one specific risk, like an earnings report, then expires. Cheap in total, but often priced with high implied volatility right before the event.</li><li><strong>Longer cover:</strong> a put several months out for a stretch of general worry. More up front, slower decay, fewer decisions.</li></ul><p>Whichever Leo picks, he should write down when the protection ends. A floor that quietly expired last Friday is not a floor.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Why might a protective put protect Leo better than a stop-loss order at the same price?`, options: [`Puts are always free`, `A put guarantees the strike price even if the stock gaps down overnight`, `Stop-losses are not allowed on stocks`, `Puts never expire`], answer: 1, explain: `A stop becomes a market order and can fill far below the stop price after a gap. A put locks in the strike until expiration.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The collar: insurance paid by upside', html: `<p>Leo winces at paying $150 every quarter. So he adds a second leg: he <strong>sells</strong> an LMNB <strong>57 call</strong> with the same expiration for <strong>$1.50</strong>.</p><p>Now he holds shares, a long 45 put, and a short 57 call. That is a <span class="term" data-def="Owning shares, buying a protective put and selling a covered call, usually so the call premium pays for the put.">collar</span>. The call premium pays for the put, so this one is a <strong>zero-cost collar</strong> (before fees).</p><ul><li><strong>Floor:</strong> $45. Max loss = ($50 - $45) x 100 = <span class="down">$500</span>.</li><li><strong>Cap:</strong> $57. Max gain = ($57 - $50) x 100 = <span class="up">$700</span>.</li></ul><p>Between $45 and $57, Leo's shares move normally. Outside that band, the options take over. He has traded away the dream of a huge rally in exchange for free protection against a crash.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'think', say: `Zero-cost collar does not mean zero cost. You pay with every dollar of upside above the call strike.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo owns shares bought at $40. He buys a 36 put for $1.00 and sells a 44 call for $0.80. What is his maximum gain at expiration, in dollars?`, answer: 380, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Net cost of the options is $1.00 - $0.80 = $0.20. Max gain is ($44 - $40 - $0.20) x 100 = $380.` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each position to its main trade-off.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Protective put', right: 'Pay premium for a floor, keep all upside' },
          { left: 'Covered call', right: 'Collect premium, cap upside, keep downside' },
          { left: 'Collar', right: 'Floor and cap, often for little net cost' },
          { left: 'Stop-loss order', right: 'Free, but can fill far below the stop on a gap' },
        ] },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Hedging vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Protective put', back: `A put bought to put a floor under shares you own.` },
          { front: 'Married put', back: `Buying shares and a protective put at the same time.` },
          { front: 'Collar', back: `Shares plus a long put plus a short call, setting both a floor and a cap.` },
          { front: 'Zero-cost collar', back: `A collar where the call premium roughly equals the put premium.` },
          { front: 'Gap', back: `A jump between one close and the next open that can skip right past a stop order.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Collars have assignment risk too', html: `<p>The short call in a collar is a covered call, with all the same rules. If the stock rallies above the strike, especially before an ex-dividend date, the shares can be called away early. If Leo has a big unrealized gain on the shares, being assigned could also trigger a taxable sale he did not plan for.</p>` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A protective put keeps protecting the shares after the put expires.`, answer: false, explain: `Protection ends at expiration. To stay protected, the investor must buy a new put, paying a new premium.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A protective put is built by...`, options: [`Owning shares and selling a put`, `Owning shares and buying a put`, `Selling shares short and buying a call`, `Buying a call and a put`], answer: 1, explain: `The long put creates a floor under shares you already own.` },
          { q: `Shares bought at $60, 55 put bought for $1.20. Breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$58.80`, `$56.20`, `$61.20`, `$55.00`], answer: 2, explain: `The stock must rise enough to pay for the put: $60 + $1.20 = $61.20.` },
          { q: `Same position: shares at $60, 55 put for $1.20. Max loss?`, options: [`$620`, `$500`, `$120`, `$6,000`], answer: 0, explain: `($60 - $55 + $1.20) x 100 = $620.` },
          { q: `In a zero-cost collar, what pays for the put?`, options: [`The broker`, `Dividends`, `Interest on cash`, `Premium from selling a call`], answer: 3, explain: `The short call's premium offsets the long put's cost, in exchange for capping upside.` },
          { q: `Why do most long-term investors avoid buying protective puts all the time?`, options: [`Puts are illegal to hold for long`, `The repeated premiums add up to a large drag on returns`, `Puts increase losses in crashes`, `Puts cannot be bought on ETFs`], answer: 1, explain: `Constant insurance is expensive. Many use it for specific, temporary risks instead.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
    {
      id: 'options-3-vertical-spreads',
      title: 'Vertical Spreads: Defined Risk',
      summary: `Buy one option, sell another at a different strike, and cap both your risk and reward. Master the four vertical spreads and their math.`,
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'scale',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Two options, one position', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="Buying one option and selling another of the same type and expiration but a different strike.">vertical spread</span> combines two options of the same type (both calls or both puts) and same expiration, but different strikes. One is bought, one is sold.</p><p>Why bother? The option you sell pays for part of the option you buy. In return, you give up gains beyond the second strike. The result is a position where <span class="hl">both maximum profit and maximum loss are known before you enter</span>.</p><p>There are four classic verticals, and they come in two families:</p><ul><li><strong>Debit spreads:</strong> you pay to open. Bull call spread and bear put spread.</li><li><strong>Credit spreads:</strong> you get paid to open. Bull put spread and bear call spread.</li></ul><p>We will use one LMNB chain for all four. Stock at <strong>$50</strong>, about 45 days to expiration: 50 call $3.00, 55 call $1.20, 60 call $0.40, 50 put $2.80, 45 put $1.00, 40 put $0.35.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Vertical spreads trade the moonshot for a smaller bill and a known worst case. Very grown-up. I approve.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'vertical-spread', caption: `A vertical spread's payoff is flat at max loss on one side, flat at max profit on the other, with a sloped ramp between the two strikes.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bull call spread (debit)', html: `<p>Leo is bullish on LMNB. He <strong>buys the 50 call for $3.00</strong> and <strong>sells the 55 call for $1.20</strong>.</p><p>Net debit: $3.00 - $1.20 = <strong>$1.80</strong>, or $180 per spread.</p><ul><li><strong>Max loss:</strong> the debit, <span class="down">$180</span>, if LMNB ends at or below $50.</li><li><strong>Max profit:</strong> width minus debit = ($5 - $1.80) x 100 = <span class="up">$320</span>, if LMNB ends at or above $55.</li><li><strong>Breakeven:</strong> long strike + debit = <strong>$51.80</strong>.</li></ul><p>Compare buying the 50 call alone for $300: breakeven $53, unlimited upside. The spread is cheaper and breaks even sooner, but caps the gain at $320. If Leo thinks LMNB lands around $55, not $70, the spread fits his forecast better.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Dev buys a 40 call for $2.50 and sells a 45 call for $0.90, same expiration. What is his maximum profit per spread, in dollars?`, answer: 340, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Debit is $2.50 - $0.90 = $1.60. Max profit is width minus debit: ($5 - $1.60) x 100 = $340.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Bear put spread (debit)', html: `<p>Dev turns bearish. He <strong>buys the 50 put for $2.80</strong> and <strong>sells the 45 put for $1.00</strong>.</p><p>Net debit: <strong>$1.80</strong>, or $180.</p><ul><li><strong>Max loss:</strong> <span class="down">$180</span>, if LMNB ends at or above $50.</li><li><strong>Max profit:</strong> ($5 - $1.80) x 100 = <span class="up">$320</span>, if LMNB ends at or below $45.</li><li><strong>Breakeven:</strong> long strike - debit = <strong>$48.20</strong>.</li></ul><p>It is the mirror image of the bull call spread. Debit spreads in general: you pay up front, the debit is your max loss, and max profit is the distance between strikes minus what you paid.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Credit spreads: getting paid first', html: `<p><strong>Bull put spread.</strong> Ms. Ortiz thinks LMNB will stay above $45. She <strong>sells the 45 put for $1.00</strong> and <strong>buys the 40 put for $0.35</strong>. Net credit: <strong>$0.65</strong>, or $65.</p><ul><li>Max profit: the credit, <span class="up">$65</span>, if LMNB stays at or above $45.</li><li>Max loss: (width - credit) x 100 = ($5 - $0.65) x 100 = <span class="down">$435</span>, at or below $40.</li><li>Breakeven: short strike - credit = <strong>$44.35</strong>.</li></ul><p><strong>Bear call spread.</strong> Leo thinks LMNB will stay below $55. He <strong>sells the 55 call for $1.20</strong> and <strong>buys the 60 call for $0.40</strong>. Net credit: <strong>$0.80</strong>.</p><ul><li>Max profit: <span class="up">$80</span>, at or below $55.</li><li>Max loss: ($5 - $0.80) x 100 = <span class="down">$420</span>, at or above $60.</li><li>Breakeven: short strike + credit = <strong>$55.80</strong>.</li></ul><p>The bought option is the safety net that turns an open-ended short option into a defined-risk position.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The four formulas', html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Spread</th><th>Max profit</th><th>Max loss</th><th>Breakeven</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bull call (debit)</td><td>Width - debit</td><td>Debit</td><td>Long strike + debit</td></tr><tr><td>Bear put (debit)</td><td>Width - debit</td><td>Debit</td><td>Long strike - debit</td></tr><tr><td>Bull put (credit)</td><td>Credit</td><td>Width - credit</td><td>Short strike - credit</td></tr><tr><td>Bear call (credit)</td><td>Credit</td><td>Width - credit</td><td>Short strike + credit</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Multiply every dollar figure by 100 per spread. "Width" is the distance between the two strikes.</p><p>A neat check: <span class="hl">max profit + max loss always equals the width</span>. For Leo's bull call spread, $1.80 + $3.20 = $5. If your numbers do not add up to the width, something is off.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo sells a 105 call for $2.00 and buys a 110 call for $0.70. What is his maximum loss per spread, in dollars?`, answer: 370, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Credit is $2.00 - $0.70 = $1.30. Max loss is width minus credit: ($5 - $1.30) x 100 = $370.` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each spread to the outlook it expresses.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Bull call spread', right: 'Bullish, pay a debit' },
          { left: 'Bear put spread', right: 'Bearish, pay a debit' },
          { left: 'Bull put spread', right: 'Bullish or neutral, collect a credit' },
          { left: 'Bear call spread', right: 'Bearish or neutral, collect a credit' },
        ] },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Debit vs credit: same coin, different face', html: `<p>Look at the risk and reward. Ms. Ortiz's bull put spread risks <strong>$435 to make $65</strong>. Leo's bull call spread risks <strong>$180 to make $320</strong>. Is one smarter?</p><p>Not automatically. Credit spreads are usually built with the short strike out of the money, so they win <em>often</em> but small. Debit spreads like Leo's win <em>less often</em> but bigger. Option prices already balance the odds against the payout. A great-looking ratio usually comes with worse odds, and vice versa.</p><p>Brokers usually require you to hold collateral equal to a credit spread's max loss, and spreads typically need a higher option approval level than buying calls and puts.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Max profit plus max loss always equals the width. When the math adds up, the risk is truly defined.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Ms. Ortiz sells a 45 put for $1.00 and buys a 40 put for $0.35. What is the breakeven stock price at expiration?`, answer: 44.35, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `For a bull put spread, breakeven is short strike minus credit: $45 - $0.65 = $44.35.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Spread-specific risks', html: `<p>Defined risk does not mean no surprises.</p><ul><li><strong>Early assignment on the short leg.</strong> If the short option goes deep in the money, it can be assigned early, especially before a dividend. Your long leg still protects you, but you may suddenly hold shares or a short stock position and need to act.</li><li><strong>Expiring between the strikes.</strong> If LMNB closes at $52 with Leo's 50/55 call spread, the 50 call is in the money and will likely be auto-exercised, while the 55 expires. Leo wakes up owning 100 shares. Most traders close spreads before expiration to avoid this.</li><li><strong>Two spreads to cross.</strong> Each leg has its own bid-ask spread. Use a single multi-leg limit order so both legs fill together at a net price you choose.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Defined risk is defined at expiration with both legs intact. Let one leg get assigned or expire alone and the math changes.` } },
        { type: 'check', question: `LMNB closes at $52 on expiration day. Leo still holds his 50/55 bull call spread. What is most likely to happen if he does nothing?`, options: [`Both options expire worthless`, `Both options are exercised`, `The 50 call is auto-exercised and he ends up owning 100 shares`, `The broker cancels the spread`], answer: 2, explain: `The 50 call is in the money and gets auto-exercised. The 55 call is out of the money and expires. He now holds shares, unhedged, over the weekend.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Buy a 30 call for $2.00, sell a 35 call for $0.60. Max loss?`, options: [`$140`, `$200`, `$360`, `$500`], answer: 0, explain: `It is a debit spread. The debit is $2.00 - $0.60 = $1.40, so max loss is $140.` },
          { q: `Same spread. Breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$30.60`, `$32.00`, `$33.60`, `$31.40`], answer: 3, explain: `Long strike plus debit: $30 + $1.40 = $31.40.` },
          { q: `Sell a 50 put for $1.50, buy a 45 put for $0.50. Max loss?`, options: [`$100`, `$400`, `$500`, `$150`], answer: 1, explain: `Credit is $1.00. Max loss is ($5 - $1.00) x 100 = $400.` },
          { q: `For any vertical spread, max profit plus max loss equals...`, options: [`The premium paid`, `Zero`, `The width between strikes`, `The stock price`], answer: 2, explain: `Per share, the two always sum to the distance between the strikes.` },
          { q: `Which spread profits if the stock stays below the short call strike?`, options: [`Bear call spread`, `Bull call spread`, `Bull put spread`, `Protective put`], answer: 0, explain: `A bear call spread collects a credit and keeps it if the stock stays at or below the short call strike.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
    {
      id: 'options-3-straddles-strangles',
      title: 'Straddles & Strangles: Betting on a Big Move',
      summary: `Buy a call and a put together to profit from a big move in either direction. Learn the breakevens, and why earnings and IV crush make this harder than it looks.`,
      minutes: 15,
      icon: 'bolt',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Direction? No idea. Size? Huge.', html: `<p>LMNB reports earnings tonight. Dev has no clue whether the news will be good or bad, but he is sure the stock will move <em>a lot</em>. Can he bet on that?</p><p>Yes. He can buy a call <strong>and</strong> a put at the same time. If LMNB rockets, the call pays. If it craters, the put pays. If it barely moves, both lose value.</p><p>When both options share the same strike, usually at the money, the position is a <span class="term" data-def="Buying a call and a put with the same strike and expiration, to profit from a big move either way.">straddle</span>. When the call strike is above the price and the put strike is below it, it is a <span class="term" data-def="Buying an out-of-the-money call and an out-of-the-money put with the same expiration. Cheaper than a straddle but needs a bigger move.">strangle</span>.</p><p>These are bets on <strong>movement, not direction</strong>. Your real opponent is not up or down. It is the size of the move the market already expects.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'wow', say: `Straddles do not care which way the stock goes. They only care how far. And how fast.` } },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'straddle', caption: `A long straddle's payoff is a V: the biggest loss is right at the strike, and profits grow as the stock moves far in either direction past the breakevens.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Straddle math', html: `<p>LMNB is at <strong>$100</strong>. Options expiring this Friday:</p><ul><li>Buy the 100 call for <strong>$4.00</strong></li><li>Buy the 100 put for <strong>$3.80</strong></li></ul><p>Total cost: <strong>$7.80</strong>, or <span class="down">$780</span>. That is the max loss, if LMNB closes Friday exactly at $100 and both options expire worthless.</p><p>Two breakevens:</p><ul><li><span class="hl">Upper = strike + total premium</span> = <strong>$107.80</strong></li><li><span class="hl">Lower = strike - total premium</span> = <strong>$92.20</strong></li></ul><p>LMNB must move more than <strong>7.8%</strong>, either way, by Friday for Dev to profit at expiration. If it jumps to $115, the call is worth $15, the put is worthless, and Dev makes $15 - $7.80 = $7.20, or <span class="up">$720</span>.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A stock is at $60. A 60 call costs $2.40 and a 60 put costs $2.10. What is the upper breakeven of the straddle at expiration?`, answer: 64.5, tolerance: 0.01, unit: '$', explain: `Total premium is $4.50. Upper breakeven is $60 + $4.50 = $64.50. The lower one is $55.50.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The strangle: cheaper, wider', html: `<p>Instead, Dev could buy the <strong>110 call for $1.50</strong> and the <strong>90 put for $1.30</strong>. Total: <strong>$2.80</strong>, or $280.</p><ul><li>Upper breakeven: call strike + premium = <strong>$112.80</strong></li><li>Lower breakeven: put strike - premium = <strong>$87.20</strong></li><li>Max loss: <span class="down">$280</span>, anywhere between $90 and $110 at expiration.</li></ul><p>The strangle costs much less, but LMNB now has to move almost <strong>13%</strong> instead of 7.8% to break even. And the zone where Dev loses everything is wide: anywhere from $90 to $110.</p><p>Same trade-off as always: cheaper options need bigger moves. The market is not handing out discounts. It is pricing a lower probability.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo buys a 55 call for $0.90 and a 45 put for $0.70 on a $50 stock. What is his maximum loss on the strangle, in dollars?`, answer: 160, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Total premium is $0.90 + $0.70 = $1.60, times 100 = $160, lost if the stock ends between $45 and $55.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The expected move is already priced', html: `<p>Remember from the IV lesson: the price of the at-the-money straddle is a rough estimate of the move the market expects. Dev's $7.80 straddle says the market is pricing about a 7.8% earnings move.</p><p>So buying the straddle is not a bet that LMNB will move. Everyone knows it will move. It is a bet that LMNB will move <strong>more than the market expects</strong>.</p><p>That is a much harder bet. Before earnings, implied volatility is pumped up, which makes both options expensive. Market makers who sell straddles study past earnings moves carefully. To win, you need a reaction bigger than what a crowd of professionals already priced in.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `The at-the-money straddle price is roughly the market's guess at the move. To profit buying it, you have to beat that guess.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'IV crush, double strength', html: `<p>Earnings come out. LMNB jumps to <strong>$105</strong>, a solid 5% move. Dev checks his straddle.</p><ul><li>The 100 call: $5 intrinsic plus a little leftover time value, about <strong>$5.20</strong>.</li><li>The 100 put: far out of the money now, about <strong>$0.15</strong>.</li></ul><p>Total: <strong>$5.35</strong>. He paid $7.80. Loss: <span class="down">$245</span>, on a 5% move.</p><p>What happened? Two things at once. The move was smaller than the 7.8% priced in, and implied volatility collapsed after the news, crushing the time value in <em>both</em> options. A straddle holds two long options, so it carries double the vega exposure to IV crush.</p><p>Many traders who buy straddles therefore enter well before the event and sell before the announcement, trying to profit from rising IV rather than from the move itself. That has its own risks: IV does not always rise as expected.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `A 5% move and still a loss. Long straddles into earnings need a move bigger than the market's own estimate, every single time.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Dev paid $7.80 for a straddle. After earnings it is worth $5.35. How many dollars did he lose on one straddle?`, answer: 245, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$7.80 - $5.35 = $2.45 per share, times 100 = $245.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The short side: selling straddles and strangles', html: `<p>Someone sold Dev those options. A <strong>short straddle</strong> collects the $780 and profits if LMNB stays near $100. Max profit is the full $780, if LMNB closes Friday exactly at $100.</p><p>The risks are serious:</p><ul><li><strong>Upside:</strong> the short call is naked, so losses are theoretically unlimited.</li><li><strong>Downside:</strong> the short put can lose up to ($100 - $7.80) x 100 = $9,220 if LMNB goes to zero.</li></ul><p>Short strangles collect less premium but give the stock more room. Both are popular with traders who sell volatility, and both can produce a long run of small wins wiped out by one surprise. Brokers usually restrict them to their highest option approval levels, with large margin requirements that can jump when markets get wild.</p>` },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Volatility trade vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Long straddle', back: `Buy a call and a put at the same strike. Profits from a big move either way.` },
          { front: 'Long strangle', back: `Buy an out-of-the-money call and put. Cheaper, but needs a bigger move.` },
          { front: 'Expected move', back: `The move implied by option prices, often estimated from the at-the-money straddle.` },
          { front: 'Short straddle', back: `Sell a call and a put at the same strike. Profits if the stock stays still, with large risk.` },
          { front: 'Two breakevens', back: `Strike or strikes plus and minus the total premium paid.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'example', title: 'Leo checks the history first', html: `<p>Before trying a straddle into earnings, Leo looks up how far LMNB actually moved after each of its last several reports and compares that with what the straddle cost each time. Sometimes the stock moved more than priced, sometimes less. Past moves do not predict the next one, but the exercise shows him that the market's estimate is usually in the right neighborhood, which makes a straddle a coin flip at best, before costs.</p>` },
        { type: 'check', question: `Which outcome is worst for a long straddle buyer?`, options: [`The stock jumps 20%`, `The stock falls 20%`, `The stock closes right at the strike on expiration day`, `Implied volatility rises sharply before expiration`], answer: 2, explain: `At the strike, both options expire worthless and the buyer loses the full premium. Big moves either way or rising IV help.` },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `A long strangle needs a bigger move to break even than a long straddle on the same stock and expiration.`, answer: true, explain: `The strangle is cheaper, but its strikes start further away, so the stock must travel further before either option pays back the total premium.` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `A long straddle is...`, options: [`Buying a call and a put with the same strike and expiration`, `Buying a call and selling a put`, `Selling a call and a put at different strikes`, `Owning stock and buying a put`], answer: 0, explain: `Both options are bought at the same strike, usually at the money.` },
          { q: `Stock at $40. 40 call $1.80, 40 put $1.60. Lower breakeven?`, options: [`$38.40`, `$36.60`, `$38.20`, `$43.40`], answer: 1, explain: `Total premium is $3.40. Lower breakeven is $40 - $3.40 = $36.60.` },
          { q: `What does a long straddle buyer really need, to profit?`, options: [`The stock to rise`, `The stock to stay flat`, `A dividend increase`, `A move larger than the one already priced in`], answer: 3, explain: `The straddle's price already reflects the expected move. Profit requires beating it.` },
          { q: `Why does IV crush hurt a long straddle so much?`, options: [`It only affects calls`, `The straddle holds two long options, so both lose time value when IV falls`, `It makes the strike move`, `It increases theta for buyers to positive`], answer: 1, explain: `Both legs have positive vega, so a drop in implied volatility hits the position twice.` },
          { q: `What is the maximum loss on a short straddle?`, options: [`The premium collected`, `Zero`, `Theoretically unlimited, because of the short call`, `Exactly the strike price`], answer: 2, explain: `The short call has no upside limit. The short put also carries large risk down to zero.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
    {
      id: 'options-3-iron-condors-butterflies',
      title: 'Iron Condors & Butterflies',
      summary: `Strategies that profit when a stock stays in a range. Learn how they are built, their defined risk, and why a high win rate can still lose money.`,
      minutes: 16,
      icon: 'target',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Betting on boring', html: `<p>Straddles bet on a big move. What if you think a stock will do almost nothing?</p><p>Ms. Ortiz has watched LMNB, now at <strong>$100</strong>, drift between $95 and $105 for months. No earnings report in the next month, no big events on the calendar. She thinks it will stay in that range.</p><p>Range-bound strategies try to collect premium from options that expire worthless when the stock stays put. The two most famous are the <strong>iron condor</strong> and the <strong>butterfly</strong>. Both have <span class="hl">defined risk</span>: max profit and max loss are known from the start.</p><p>They also have a reputation for "high win rates." By the end of this lesson, you will see why that phrase can be misleading.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'chip', mood: 'happy', say: `Some days the most exciting stock is the one that does nothing. These strategies get paid for that.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Building an iron condor', html: `<p>An <span class="term" data-def="A bull put spread plus a bear call spread on the same stock and expiration, profiting if the price stays between the short strikes.">iron condor</span> is two credit spreads glued together: a bull put spread below the price and a bear call spread above it. With LMNB at $100, about 30 days out:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sell 95 put</td><td>+$1.20</td></tr><tr><td>Buy 90 put</td><td>-$0.50</td></tr><tr><td>Sell 105 call</td><td>+$1.30</td></tr><tr><td>Buy 110 call</td><td>-$0.55</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Net credit</strong></td><td><strong>$1.45</strong></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ms. Ortiz collects <strong>$145</strong>. If LMNB stays between $95 and $105 until expiration, all four options expire worthless and she keeps it all.</p><p>The bought options (90 put and 110 call) are the wings. They cap the loss if LMNB breaks out of the range.</p>` },
        { type: 'diagram', name: 'iron-condor', caption: `The iron condor's payoff is a flat-topped plateau of max profit between the short strikes, sloping down to flat max losses beyond the long strikes on either side.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Iron condor math', html: `<ul><li><span class="hl">Max profit = net credit</span> = <span class="up">$145</span>, if LMNB ends between $95 and $105.</li><li><span class="hl">Max loss = wing width - net credit</span> = ($5 - $1.45) x 100 = <span class="down">$355</span>, if LMNB ends at or below $90 or at or above $110.</li><li><strong>Lower breakeven:</strong> short put - credit = <strong>$93.55</strong>.</li><li><strong>Upper breakeven:</strong> short call + credit = <strong>$106.45</strong>.</li></ul><p>Why is max loss only one wing's worth? Because LMNB cannot finish both below $90 and above $110. At expiration, at most one side can lose. That is also why brokers typically require collateral equal to one side's max loss, not both.</p><p>The profit zone, from $93.55 to $106.45, is about 13% wide. That feels comfortable. Hold that thought.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `An iron condor uses $5-wide wings and collects a net credit of $1.20. What is its maximum loss per condor, in dollars?`, answer: 380, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Wing width minus credit: ($5 - $1.20) x 100 = $380.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Why a high win rate can still lose', html: `<p>Suppose Ms. Ortiz trades this exact condor 10 times. Purely as a hypothetical, say 7 times LMNB stays in range and she keeps the full $145, and 3 times it breaks out and she takes the full $355 loss.</p><ul><li>Wins: 7 x $145 = <span class="up">$1,015</span></li><li>Losses: 3 x $355 = <span class="down">$1,065</span></li><li>Net: <span class="down">-$50</span>, before fees on four legs per trade</li></ul><p>A 70% win rate, and she still lost money. When each loss is more than twice the size of each win, you need to win much more often than 70% just to break even. Here, she would need to win about 71% of the time, and that is before commissions and spreads.</p><p>Option prices tend to bake this in. The higher the probability of a win, the smaller the credit compared with the risk. <span class="hl">Win rate alone tells you almost nothing. You need win rate and the size of wins and losses together.</span></p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Seven wins, three losses, net loss. Anyone bragging about win rate without mentioning loss size is telling half the story.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A strategy wins $100 per trade 80% of the time and loses $400 per trade 20% of the time. Over 10 typical trades, what is the net result in dollars?`, answer: 0, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `8 wins x $100 = $800. 2 losses x $400 = $800. Net $0, before fees. An 80% win rate only breaks even here.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Butterflies: a bullseye trade', html: `<p>A <span class="term" data-def="A three-strike options position, such as buying one low call, selling two middle calls and buying one high call, that profits most if the stock ends at the middle strike.">butterfly</span> targets one specific price. A long call butterfly with LMNB at $100:</p><ul><li>Buy 1 95 call at <strong>$6.80</strong></li><li>Sell 2 100 calls at <strong>$3.50</strong> each (+$7.00)</li><li>Buy 1 105 call at <strong>$1.30</strong></li></ul><p>Net debit: $6.80 + $1.30 - $7.00 = <strong>$1.10</strong>, or $110.</p><ul><li><strong>Max loss:</strong> <span class="down">$110</span>, if LMNB ends at or below $95 or at or above $105.</li><li><strong>Max profit:</strong> (wing width - debit) x 100 = ($5 - $1.10) x 100 = <span class="up">$390</span>, only if LMNB closes exactly at $100.</li><li><strong>Breakevens:</strong> $96.10 and $103.90.</li></ul><p>Cheap, defined risk, attractive reward. The catch: max profit needs a near bullseye at expiration, and the profit zone is narrow.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `A call butterfly uses strikes 45, 50 and 55 and costs a net debit of $0.80. What is its maximum profit per butterfly, in dollars?`, answer: 420, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `Wing width is $5. Max profit is ($5 - $0.80) x 100 = $420, reached if the stock closes exactly at $50.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Iron butterflies and managing range trades', html: `<p>An <strong>iron butterfly</strong> is an iron condor with both short strikes at the same price, for example sell the 100 put and 100 call, buy the 95 put and 105 call. It collects a bigger credit than a condor but has a much narrower profit zone.</p><p>Managing these trades matters as much as setting them up:</p><ul><li><strong>Close early.</strong> Many traders take profits well before expiration instead of waiting for the last dollar, because gamma risk near expiration can flip a winner fast.</li><li><strong>Watch the short strikes.</strong> If the stock approaches one, the risk of a max loss grows quickly.</li><li><strong>Mind the legs.</strong> Four legs means four bid-ask spreads. On illiquid options, the spreads alone can eat a big chunk of the credit.</li><li><strong>Assignment still applies.</strong> A short leg that goes in the money can be assigned, which breaks the neat structure.</li></ul>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Condors and butterflies were named for their payoff shapes. A wide body with wings, or a narrow body with wings.` } },
        { type: 'cards', title: 'Range trade vocabulary', cards: [
          { front: 'Iron condor', back: `Short put spread plus short call spread. Max profit if the stock stays between the short strikes.` },
          { front: 'Wings', back: `The bought options that cap losses on each side.` },
          { front: 'Long butterfly', back: `Buy one low strike, sell two middle, buy one high. Peaks at the middle strike.` },
          { front: 'Iron butterfly', back: `An iron condor whose short put and short call share the same strike.` },
          { front: 'Win rate', back: `How often a trade wins. Meaningless without the size of wins and losses.` },
        ] },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'warn', title: 'Calm stocks do not stay calm on schedule', html: `<p>Range trades often look safest on stocks that have been quiet. But a surprise merger announcement, lawsuit or market-wide selloff does not check the calendar first. Quiet stretches are exactly when option premiums are cheap, which means the credit collected may be small compared with the risk of a sudden breakout.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each strategy to its view.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Iron condor', right: 'Stock stays inside a range' },
          { left: 'Long butterfly', right: 'Stock ends near one target price' },
          { left: 'Long straddle', right: 'Stock moves a lot either way' },
          { left: 'Bull call spread', right: 'Stock rises moderately' },
        ] },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `An iron condor combines...`, options: [`Two debit spreads`, `A covered call and a protective put`, `A bull put spread and a bear call spread`, `A long straddle and a short strangle`], answer: 2, explain: `It sells a put spread below the price and a call spread above it, collecting two credits.` },
          { q: `An iron condor sells the 45 put and 55 call for a total net credit of $1.60. Its breakevens are...`, options: [`$43.40 and $56.60`, `$45 and $55`, `$46.60 and $53.40`, `$40 and $60`], answer: 0, explain: `Short put minus credit, and short call plus credit: $45 - $1.60 = $43.40 and $55 + $1.60 = $56.60.` },
          { q: `Why is an iron condor's max loss only one wing's width minus the credit?`, options: [`Brokers waive the other side`, `The credit covers the other side`, `Puts cannot lose money`, `The stock cannot finish beyond both wings at once`], answer: 3, explain: `At expiration the stock is either below the put side, above the call side, or between them. Only one side can lose.` },
          { q: `Where does a long call butterfly reach max profit?`, options: [`Far above the top strike`, `Exactly at the middle strike`, `Far below the bottom strike`, `Anywhere between the outer strikes equally`], answer: 1, explain: `The two short middle calls make the payoff peak at the middle strike.` },
          { q: `A strategy has a 75% win rate. What else must you know to judge it?`, options: [`The average size of wins compared with losses`, `Nothing, 75% is clearly good`, `Only the stock's dividend yield`, `The day of the week trades are placed`], answer: 0, explain: `If losses are much bigger than wins, a high win rate can still lose money overall.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
    {
      id: 'options-3-danger-zone',
      title: 'The Danger Zone',
      summary: `Grizz walks through the ways options go wrong: approval levels, 0DTE, naked calls, assignment surprises, wide spreads, taxes and the risk document every broker hands you.`,
      minutes: 17,
      icon: 'warning',
      steps: [
        { type: 'read', title: 'Grizz takes the mic', html: `<p>You now know more about options than most people who trade them. That is exactly why this lesson exists.</p><p>Options are powerful tools. They can insure a portfolio, generate income and express precise views. They can also wipe out an account in an afternoon. The difference is rarely intelligence. It is usually one of a handful of predictable mistakes: too much leverage, a position with no loss limit, a surprise at expiration, or a trade nobody fully read the fine print on.</p><p>None of this is meant to scare you away. It is meant to make sure that if you ever do trade options, you walk in knowing where the trapdoors are. Grizz has seen every one of them.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `My job is to ask the annoying question before every trade: what is the worst thing that can happen, in dollars?` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Brokers make you ask permission', html: `<p>You cannot just open a brokerage account and start selling naked calls. In the US, you generally must be an adult (18 or older in most states) to open your own account, and most brokers do not allow options trading in custodial accounts for minors. That is why Maya, at 17, practices with a paper-trading simulator instead.</p><p>To trade options at all, you apply for <span class="term" data-def="The broker's permission tier that decides which option strategies an account may use.">options approval</span>. The application asks about your experience, income, net worth and goals. The broker then assigns a level. Each broker defines its own tiers, but a typical setup looks like this:</p><ul><li><strong>Lower levels:</strong> covered calls and cash-secured puts.</li><li><strong>Middle levels:</strong> buying calls and puts, then spreads.</li><li><strong>Highest level:</strong> uncovered (naked) option selling, usually with a margin account and stricter requirements.</li></ul><p>Being denied a level is not an insult. It is a guardrail.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The document nobody reads', html: `<p>Before approving an account for options, US brokers are required to give you a booklet called <strong>Characteristics and Risks of Standardized Options</strong>, usually called the <span class="term" data-def="Options Disclosure Document: the OCC booklet, Characteristics and Risks of Standardized Options, that brokers must provide to options customers.">Options Disclosure Document (ODD)</span>. It is published by the OCC and updated over time.</p><p>Most people click past it. Do not. It explains, in careful legal language, how exercise and assignment work, how adjusted contracts behave, and many of the risks in this lesson. When the ODD is updated, brokers send the supplements too.</p><p>It is long and dry. It is also the official description of the product you are trading. Reading even the risk sections puts you ahead of most beginners.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'hoot', mood: 'think', say: `Fun fact: the ODD is required reading the broker hands you for free. Less fun fact: almost nobody reads it.` } },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Leverage, honestly', html: `<p>Leverage is the feature everyone loves and the reason most blowups happen.</p><p>Dev puts <strong>$2,000</strong> into short-dated LMNB calls. A 3% rise in the stock might double them. A 3% drop, or simply a quiet week, can cut them in half or wipe them out. Owning $2,000 of the stock, the same moves would mean a $60 gain or loss.</p><p>The trap is that leverage feels fine until it does not. A few wins encourage bigger positions, and bigger positions mean one bad week can erase months. When you buy options, the defined max loss is only protection if the amount you put in is small enough to lose completely. When you sell options, there may be no defined max loss at all.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Naked calls: no ceiling', html: `<p>Dev decides selling options is easier. He sells <strong>10 naked LMNB 55 calls</strong> at $1.20 each, collecting <span class="up">$1,200</span>. LMNB is at $50, and he figures it will never get to $55 in a month.</p><p>Two weeks later, a bigger company announces it will buy LMNB for <strong>$90 a share</strong>. The stock jumps there overnight. Each call is now worth at least $35.</p><p>Loss per contract: ($90 - $55 - $1.20) x 100 = <span class="down">$3,380</span>. Times 10 contracts: <span class="down">$33,800</span>. He collected $1,200 and lost more than 28 times that, in one night, with no chance to react.</p><p>As a stock rises, a naked call seller's margin requirement can rise too. If the account cannot cover it, the broker can close positions without asking, at whatever the price is at that moment. There is no upper limit to how high a stock can go, which is why a naked call's risk is described as <strong>theoretically unlimited</strong>.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `Collect $1,200, lose $33,800. Naked calls feel safe right up until a buyout, a squeeze or a surprise headline.` } },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `Leo sells one naked 40 call for $0.80. The stock gaps to $70. What is his loss on the contract, in dollars?`, answer: 2920, tolerance: 1, unit: '$', explain: `The call is worth $30. He collected $0.80. Loss: ($30 - $0.80) x 100 = $2,920.` },
        { type: 'read', title: '0DTE: options on fast-forward', html: `<p><span class="term" data-def="Zero days to expiration: options traded on the same day they expire.">0DTE</span> means "zero days to expiration": options bought or sold on the very day they expire. Products like SPX and big ETFs like SPY and QQQ have expirations every weekday, so 0DTE trades are possible daily, and they have become hugely popular in recent years.</p><p>Everything you learned about Greeks gets extreme on the last day:</p><ul><li><strong>Gamma is enormous</strong> near the money. A small move can swing an option from nearly worthless to valuable and back within an hour.</li><li><strong>Theta is brutal.</strong> Whatever time value is left disappears by the close.</li><li><strong>Sellers</strong> collecting small premiums can face losses many times larger within minutes.</li></ul><p>A $50 0DTE option can become $500 or $0 before lunch. That makes it exciting, which is exactly the problem. It rewards fast reactions and punishes hesitation, and hesitation is human.</p>`,
          sprite: { who: 'grizz', mood: 'warn', say: `0DTE compresses a month of option drama into a few hours. Small premiums do not mean small risk.` } },
        { type: 'truefalse', statement: `0DTE options are low risk because their premiums are usually small.`, answer: false, explain: `A small premium can still go to zero within hours, and sellers face sharp losses because gamma is extreme near expiration. Low price is not low risk.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Assignment surprises, the greatest hits', html: `<p>From Chapter 1 and this chapter, here are the expiration and assignment traps that catch people most often:</p><ul><li><strong>The one-cent rule:</strong> an option in the money by $0.01 or more at expiration is usually auto-exercised. Suddenly you own, or are short, 100 shares per contract.</li><li><strong>After-hours moves:</strong> holders can decide after the close, so a short option that looked safe at 4:00 p.m. can still be assigned.</li><li><strong>Dividends:</strong> in-the-money short calls get assigned early right before ex-dividend dates.</li><li><strong>Broken spreads:</strong> one leg of a spread gets assigned or expires in the money alone, leaving you with an unhedged stock position.</li><li><strong>Not enough cash:</strong> if an exercise needs money you do not have, the broker may close your position at a bad price or issue a margin call.</li></ul><p>The fix is boring and reliable: close or roll positions before expiration day, especially anything near the strike.</p>` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'The spread tax on illiquid options', html: `<p>Popular contracts often trade with spreads of a few cents. Obscure ones can be shocking.</p><p>Picture a small-company option with a bid of <strong>$0.80</strong> and an ask of <strong>$1.40</strong>. Buy at the ask and you need the option to rise more than 40% just to sell at the bid for what you paid. A round trip at market prices costs $0.60 x 100 = <span class="down">$60</span> per contract before the stock moves at all.</p><p>Warning signs: low open interest, little or no volume, and a spread that is a large percentage of the option's price. Multi-leg strategies multiply the problem, since every leg has its own spread.</p><p>Limit orders help, but they cannot create buyers who are not there. If you might need to exit quickly, liquidity is part of your risk.</p>` },
        { type: 'numeric', question: `An option has a bid of $0.80 and an ask of $1.40. If Dev buys one contract at the ask and immediately sells at the bid, how many dollars does he lose?`, answer: 60, tolerance: 0.5, unit: '$', explain: `$1.40 - $0.80 = $0.60 per share, times 100 = $60, before fees.` },
        { type: 'read', title: 'Taxes: the basics', html: `<p>This is general education, not tax advice, and tax rules change. For US taxpayers, the broad strokes are:</p><ul><li>Option profits and losses are generally <strong>capital gains and losses</strong>. Most option trades last under a year, so gains are usually <strong>short-term</strong>, taxed at ordinary income rates, which are typically higher than long-term rates.</li><li>An option that expires worthless is a capital loss for the buyer and generally a short-term gain for the seller.</li><li><strong>Broad-based index options</strong> such as SPX are usually <strong>Section 1256 contracts</strong>: gains are treated as 60% long-term and 40% short-term no matter how long you held them, and open positions are marked to market at year end.</li><li>The <strong>wash sale rule</strong> can apply when options and the underlying stock are traded around a loss.</li></ul><p>Your broker reports trades on Form 1099-B. For anything beyond the basics, check current IRS guidance or ask a tax professional.</p>` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'fact', title: 'The pattern day trader rule is changing', html: `<p>For years, FINRA's rule said that making four or more day trades within five business days in a margin account (when they are more than 6% of your trades) could label you a pattern day trader, requiring at least <strong>$25,000</strong> in account equity to keep day trading. Regulators have been working on replacing that rule, so check FINRA's and your broker's current requirements before relying on any number.</p>` },
        { type: 'match', prompt: `Match each risk to what it looks like.`, pairs: [
          { left: 'Naked call', right: 'Losses with no upper limit if the stock soars' },
          { left: '0DTE', right: 'Huge gamma and full decay within hours' },
          { left: 'Illiquid option', right: 'A wide bid-ask spread costs you on entry and exit' },
          { left: 'Expiration pin', right: 'Unsure whether you will be assigned over the weekend' },
        ] },
        { type: 'check', question: `Which document must US brokers provide to customers before approving them for options trading?`, options: [`A prospectus for each stock`, `The company's annual report`, `Characteristics and Risks of Standardized Options`, `The exchange's fee schedule`], answer: 2, explain: `The Options Disclosure Document, published by the OCC, explains how options work and their risks. Brokers must deliver it to options customers.` },
        { type: 'callout', variant: 'tip', title: `Grizz's pre-trade checklist`, html: `<p>Before any options trade, answer these out loud: <strong>1.</strong> What is my maximum loss, in dollars? <strong>2.</strong> Could I lose it all without real damage to my life? <strong>3.</strong> What happens if I am still holding this at expiration? <strong>4.</strong> Is the bid-ask spread reasonable? <strong>5.</strong> What is my exit plan, and when? If any answer is "I am not sure," the trade can wait.</p>` },
        { type: 'quiz', questions: [
          { q: `Dev sells 5 naked 30 calls for $0.50 each. The stock jumps to $45. What is his total loss?`, options: [`$250`, `$7,250`, `$7,500`, `$1,450`], answer: 1, explain: `Each call is worth $15. Loss per contract: ($15 - $0.50) x 100 = $1,450. Times 5 = $7,250.` },
          { q: `Why do brokers assign options approval levels?`, options: [`To charge higher fees`, `To limit riskier strategies to accounts that qualify for them`, `Because the SEC sets one level for everyone`, `To decide which stocks you may buy`], answer: 1, explain: `Approval tiers match strategy risk to the customer's experience and finances. Each broker sets its own tiers.` },
          { q: `What is generally true about gains on broad-based index options like SPX for US taxpayers?`, options: [`They are tax-free`, `They are always long-term`, `They are always short-term`, `They are usually taxed as 60% long-term and 40% short-term`], answer: 3, explain: `Most broad-based index options are Section 1256 contracts with 60/40 treatment, regardless of holding period.` },
          { q: `What makes 0DTE options especially risky?`, options: [`Extreme gamma and rapid time decay on expiration day`, `They cannot be sold before the close`, `They have unlimited premium`, `They are not cleared by the OCC`], answer: 0, explain: `On the final day, prices can swing wildly with small moves and all remaining time value vanishes.` },
          { q: `What is the most reliable way to avoid most expiration-day assignment surprises?`, options: [`Hope the stock stays put`, `Sell more options at the same strike`, `Close or roll positions before expiration, especially near the strike`, `Switch to a different broker on Friday`], answer: 2, explain: `Closing positions ahead of time removes auto-exercise, pin risk and after-hours surprises.` },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  bossQuestions: [
    { q: `Ms. Ortiz buys 100 shares at $70 and sells a 75 call for $1.50. What is her maximum profit at expiration?`, options: [`$150`, `$500`, `$650`, `Unlimited`], answer: 2, explain: `($75 - $70) + $1.50 = $6.50 per share, times 100 = $650. Gains above $75 go to the call buyer.` },
    { q: `Leo buys a 60 put for $3.20 and sells a 55 put for $1.40, same expiration. What is the breakeven at expiration?`, options: [`$58.20`, `$56.80`, `$61.80`, `$55.00`], answer: 0, explain: `It is a bear put spread with a $1.80 debit. Breakeven is long strike minus debit: $60 - $1.80 = $58.20.` },
    { q: `An iron condor sells the 90 put and 110 call, buys the 85 put and 115 call, and collects $1.75 in total. What is its maximum loss?`, options: [`$175`, `$500`, `$675`, `$325`], answer: 3, explain: `Wing width $5 minus credit $1.75 = $3.25, times 100 = $325. Only one side can lose at expiration.` },
    { q: `Leo owns shares bought at $50, a long 45 put and a short 57 call, both bought and sold for $1.50. LMNB finishes at $62. What is his total result?`, options: [`+$1,200`, `+$700`, `+$550`, `+$850`], answer: 1, explain: `The option premiums cancel out. His shares are called away at $57, so he gains ($57 - $50) x 100 = $700.` },
    { q: `Which of these positions has theoretically unlimited risk?`, options: [`A bull call spread`, `An iron condor`, `A short strangle`, `A protective put`], answer: 2, explain: `A short strangle includes a naked short call, which has no loss ceiling. The others all have defined maximum losses.` },
  ],
};
